[31]}]}],"verse":14},{"anchor":"the youth who succeeded the king","refs":["2 Sam 15:6"],"sourceRowNumber":31470,"targets":[{"label":"2 Sam 15:6","passages":[{"book":"2SA","chapter":15,"verses":[6]}]}],"verse":15},{"anchor":"no limit to all the people","refs":["2 Sam 15:12-13","1 Kgs 1:5-7,40","1 Kgs 12:10-16"],"sourceRowNumber":31471,"targets":[{"label":"2 Sam 15:12-13","passages":[{"book":"2SA","chapter":15,"verses":[12,13]}]},{"label":"1 Kgs 1:5-7,40","passages":[{"book":"1KI","chapter":1,"verses":[5,6,7,40]}]},{"label":"1 Kgs 12:10-16","passages":[{"book":"1KI","chapter":12,"verses":[10,11,12,13,14,15,16]}]}],"verse":16},{"anchor":"the successor will not be celebrated by those who come even later","refs":["Judg 9:19-20","2 Sam 18:7-8","2 Sam 19:9"],"sourceRowNumber":31472,"targets":[{"label":"Judg 9:19-20","passages":[{"book":"JDG","chapter":9,"verses":[19,20]}]},{"label":"2 Sam 18:7-8","passages":[{"book":"2SA","chapter":18,"verses":[7,8]}]},{"label":"2 Sam 19:9","passages":[{"book":"2SA","chapter":19,"verses":[9]}]}],"verse":16},{"anchor":"This too is futile and a pursuit of the wind","refs":["Eccl 1:14","Eccl 2:11,17,26"],"sourceRowNumber":31473,"targets":[{"label":"Eccl 1:14","passages":[{"book":"ECC","chapter":1,"verses":[14]}]},{"label":"Eccl 2:11,17,26","passages":[{"book":"ECC","chapter":2,"verses":[11,17,26]}]}],"verse":16}],"dictionaryArticles":{"Age":{"file":"dictionary/6041f3ce18b492a0c52cd3c158a8b0f91badd705326817b7e35b68825df1a3c8.json","title":"Age"},"EcclesiastesBookof":{"file":"dictionary/1edad5b05801da0d1228eaf8dd84efeca5e25d294b555d61fa9e778a6149c8da.json","title":"Ecclesiastes, Book of"}},"dictionaryRelated":[{"id":"Age","title":"Age"},{"id":"EcclesiastesBookof","title":"Ecclesiastes, Book of"}],"dictionaryWords":[],"format":"bsb-study-chapter-v2","notes":[{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":["All Is “Vapor”"],"sourceClass":"theme-title","t":"h"},{"c":["A key word in Ecclesiastes is the Hebrew term ",{"c":["hebel"],"sourceClass":"hebrew","t":"span"}," (“vapor,” often translated “meaningless”). This word expresses the core of the Teacher’s judgment concerning life in this world."],"sourceClass":"theme-body-fl","t":"p"},{"c":["At its root, ",{"c":["hebel"],"sourceClass":"hebrew","t":"span"}," means physical “breath” or “vapor.” ",{"c":["Hebel"],"sourceClass":"hebrew","t":"span"}," is used seventy-eight times in the Old Testament, but in only three instances is the physical meaning clearly intended (",{"href":"?bref=Ps.62.9","r":"Ps.62.9","rawR":"Ps.62.9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.62.9","value":"Ps.62.9"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ps 62:9"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Pr.21.6","r":"Pr.21.6","rawR":"Pr.21.6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Pr.21.6","value":"Pr.21.6"}],"t":"ref","v":"Prov 21:6"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Isa.57.13","r":"Isa.57.13","rawR":"Isa.57.13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Isa.57.13","value":"Isa.57.13"}],"t":"ref","v":"Isa 57:13"},"). In the other seventy-five instances, the word is used metaphorically to describe what is incomprehensible, futile, meaningless, false, transitory, or insubstantial. The term is often used to describe the insubstantiality, unreality, and worthlessness of false gods (",{"href":"?bref=Deut.32.21","r":"Deut.32.21","rawR":"Deut.32.21","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.32.21","value":"Deut.32.21"}],"t":"ref","v":"Deut 32:21"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=2Kgs.17.15","r":"2Kgs.17.15","rawR":"2Kgs.17.15","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Kgs.17.15","value":"2Kgs.17.15"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Kgs 17:15"},"). In this sense, ",{"c":["hebel"],"sourceClass":"hebrew","t":"span"}," is the opposite of “glory”—the substantial, weighty, and lasting presence of God. Sometimes the word ",{"c":["hebel"],"sourceClass":"hebrew","t":"span"}," stands for the way life is fleeting and momentary, like vapor (",{"href":"?bref=Job.7.16","r":"Job.7.16","rawR":"Job.7.16","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.7.16","value":"Job.7.16"}],"t":"ref","v":"Job 7:16"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Ps.144.4","r":"Ps.144.4","rawR":"Ps.144.4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.144.4","value":"Ps.144.4"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ps 144:4"},"). In other cases, it refers to the meaninglessness and frustration of life (",{"href":"?bref=Ps.78.33","r":"Ps.78.33","rawR":"Ps.78.33","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.78.33","value":"Ps.78.33"}],"t":"ref","v":"Pss 78:33"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Ps.94.11","r":"Ps.94.11","rawR":"Ps.94.11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.94.11","value":"Ps.94.11"}],"t":"ref","v":"94:11"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Isa.49.4","r":"Isa.49.4","rawR":"Isa.49.4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Isa.49.4","value":"Isa.49.4"}],"t":"ref","v":"Isa 49:4"},")."],"sourceClass":"theme-body","t":"p"},{"c":["There is a long tradition of understanding ",{"c":["hebel"],"sourceClass":"hebrew","t":"span"}," in Ecclesiastes as meaning “vanity,” not in the sense of thinking too highly of oneself, but in the sense of experiencing life as futile and without purpose or meaning. The ancient Greek translation of the Old Testament used the word ",{"c":["mataiotēs"],"sourceClass":"greek","t":"span"},{"c":[","],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," which means “emptiness” or “futility.” The Latin Vulgate then translated ",{"c":["hebel"],"sourceClass":"hebrew","t":"span"}," with the Latin word ",{"c":["vanitas"],"sourceClass":"latin","t":"span"},", which means “purposelessness, pointlessness, hollowness, worthlessness.” From the Vulgate comes the long-standing tradition of translating the word as “vanity” in English. Most translators today understand the word ",{"c":["hebel"],"sourceClass":"hebrew","t":"span"}," in this sense: Life on earth has no apparent purpose or meaning, and all that we do is futile."],"sourceClass":"theme-body","t":"p"},{"c":["Some translators, however, understand ",{"c":["hebel"],"sourceClass":"hebrew","t":"span"}," in Ecclesiastes to be referring primarily to the transitory impermanence of life. Vapor has no permanence or weight. This translation leads to a different understanding of Ecclesiastes: Life is not so much meaningless and futile as it is impermanent and fleeting. Many phrases in Ecclesiastes allude to the fleeting nature of human life and its efforts. In the New Testament, a similar thought can be found in ",{"href":"?bref=Jas.4.13-15","r":"Jas.4.13-15","rawR":"Jas.4.13-15","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Jas.4.13-15","value":"Jas.4.13-15"}],"t":"ref","v":"James 4:13-15"},". The understanding of ",{"c":["hebel"],"sourceClass":"hebrew","t":"span"}," as “impermanence” in Ecclesiastes has merit in explaining the book coherently; it is, however, a minority view among scholars."],"sourceClass":"theme-body","t":"p"},{"c":["The NLT translates ",{"c":["hebel"],"sourceClass":"hebrew","t":"span"}," in Ecclesiastes as “meaningless.” However, while reading Ecclesiastes, it is helpful to keep in mind the root of the word (“breath” or “vapor”) that stands behind the metaphors."],"sourceClass":"theme-body","t":"p"},{"c":["The brevity, frustration, and apparent meaninglessness of life can be depressing. This discouragement should motivate us toward working more wisely and diligently and enjoying life while we can. We have all observed the apparent absurdity of life’s tragedies, such as the success of a wicked fool or unchecked injustice. But these things come into proportion when we remember God’s sovereignty, because “God will judge us for everything we do, including every secret thing, whether good or bad” (",{"href":"?bref=Eccl.12.14","r":"Eccl.12.14","rawR":"Eccl.12.14","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Eccl.12.14","value":"Eccl.12.14"}],"t":"ref","v":"Eccl 12:14"},")."],"sourceClass":"theme-body","t":"p"},{"c":["Passages for Further Study"],"sourceClass":"theme-refs-title","t":"h"},{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Deut.32.21","r":"Deut.32.21","rawR":"Deut.32.21","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.32.21","value":"Deut.32.21"}],"t":"ref","v":"Deut 32:21"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=2Kgs.17.15","r":"2Kgs.17.15","rawR":"2Kgs.17.15","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Kgs.17.15","value":"2Kgs.17.15"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Kgs 17:15"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Job.7.16","r":"Job.7.16","rawR":"Job.7.16","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.7.16","value":"Job.7.16"}],"t":"ref","v":"Job 7:16"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Ps.62.9","r":"Ps.62.9","rawR":"Ps.62.9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.62.9","value":"Ps.62.9"}],"t":"ref","v":"Pss 62:9"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Ps.78.33","r":"Ps.78.33","rawR":"Ps.78.33","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.78.33","value":"Ps.78.33"}],"t":"ref","v":"78:33"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Ps.94.11","r":"Ps.94.11","rawR":"Ps.94.11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.94.11","value":"Ps.94.11"}],"t":"ref","v":"94:11"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Ps.144.4","r":"Ps.144.4","rawR":"Ps.144.4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.144.4","value":"Ps.144.4"}],"t":"ref","v":"144:4"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Pr.21.6","r":"Pr.21.6","rawR":"Pr.21.6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Pr.21.6","value":"Pr.21.6"}],"t":"ref","v":"Prov 21:6"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Eccl.1.2","r":"Eccl.1.2","rawR":"Eccl.1.2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Eccl.1.2","value":"Eccl.1.2"}],"t":"ref","v":"Eccl 1:2"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Isa.30.7","r":"Isa.30.7","rawR":"Isa.30.7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Isa.30.7","value":"Isa.30.7"}],"t":"ref","v":"Isa 30:7"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Isa.49.4","r":"Isa.49.4","rawR":"Isa.49.4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Isa.49.4","value":"Isa.49.4"}],"t":"ref","v":"49:4"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Isa.57.13","r":"Isa.57.13","rawR":"Isa.57.13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Isa.57.13","value":"Isa.57.13"}],"t":"ref","v":"57:13"}],"sourceClass":"theme-refs","t":"p"}],"id":"ThemeNote:AllIsVapor","refs":[{"book":"ECC","endChapter":9,"endVerse":12,"startChapter":1,"startVerse":2}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"AllIsVapor","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"ThemeNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"ECC","sourceChapterCoverage":[1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"8e44b49b8362e87e4c68edc1d9dd9f34067c57242bf842b4cc95d531232e695c","sourceItemName":"AllIsVapor","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/ThemeNotes.xml","sourceOrder":135,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"ECC","canonicalOSIS":"Eccl","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Eccl"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Eccl.1.2-9.12","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":"All Is “Vapor”","supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"All Is “Vapor”","type":"ThemeNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Eccl.3.16-5.7","r":"Eccl.3.16-5.7","rawR":"Eccl.3.16-5.7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Eccl.3.16-5.7","value":"Eccl.3.16-5.7"}],"t":"ref","v":"3:16–5:7"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," This section comments on a variety of topics, including justice, humans and animals, oppression, moderation, companionship, politics, and worship."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Eccl.3.16-5.7","refs":[{"book":"ECC","endChapter":5,"endVerse":7,"startChapter":3,"startVerse":16}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Eccl.3.16-5.7","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"ECC","sourceChapterCoverage":[3,4,5],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"958727e5b81a58a1a7f726b3106be2ca6b3396fd49ced3ce9533c999d46c8d05","sourceItemName":"Eccl.3.16-5.7","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":8000,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"ECC","canonicalOSIS":"Eccl","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Eccl"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Eccl.3.16-5.7","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Eccl.3.16-5.7","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Eccl.4.1","r":"Eccl.4.1","rawR":"Eccl.4.1","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Eccl.4.1","value":"Eccl.4.1"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:1"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["all the oppression:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," Abuse is rampant at many levels in society—in family, religion, the workplace, government, and foreign powers."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Eccl.4.1","refs":[{"book":"ECC","endChapter":4,"endVerse":1,"startChapter":4,"startVerse":1}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Eccl.4.1","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"ECC","sourceChapterCoverage":[4],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"ea5a20d8a19ecb093124ca051b1cc7d97db85a8aa4527c17eedb96fc90374e9d","sourceItemName":"Eccl.4.1","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":8007,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"ECC","canonicalOSIS":"Eccl","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Eccl"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Eccl.4.1","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Eccl.4.1","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Eccl.4.2","r":"Eccl.4.2","rawR":"Eccl.4.2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Eccl.4.2","value":"Eccl.4.2"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:2"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," Where oppression reigns, ",{"c":["the dead are better off than the living"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},". Those who have died are no longer mistreated."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Eccl.4.2","refs":[{"book":"ECC","endChapter":4,"endVerse":2,"startChapter":4,"startVerse":2}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Eccl.4.2","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"ECC","sourceChapterCoverage":[4],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"08b281aba04d42a74f3ddfd9f8fba252e8ad973e88b8d4479141e2b970830c0c","sourceItemName":"Eccl.4.2","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":8008,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"ECC","canonicalOSIS":"Eccl","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Eccl"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Eccl.4.2","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Eccl.4.2","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Eccl.4.4","r":"Eccl.4.4","rawR":"Eccl.4.4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Eccl.4.4","value":"Eccl.4.4"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:4"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["Most people are motivated to success"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," by a competitive drive. Since the fruits of these efforts have no intrinsic value, the Teacher recommends that moderation (",{"href":"?bref=Eccl.4.5-6","r":"Eccl.4.5-6","rawR":"Eccl.4.5-6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Eccl.4.5-6","value":"Eccl.4.5-6"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:5-6"},") replace envy."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Eccl.4.4","refs":[{"book":"ECC","endChapter":4,"endVerse":4,"startChapter":4,"startVerse":4}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Eccl.4.4","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"ECC","sourceChapterCoverage":[4],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"1ba468c6dba1c13cc4088e67678a71213361770dffc6bd3dd6bd5013eb86e5f5","sourceItemName":"Eccl.4.4","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":8009,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"ECC","canonicalOSIS":"Eccl","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Eccl"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Eccl.4.4","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Eccl.4.4","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Eccl.4.5-6","r":"Eccl.4.5-6","rawR":"Eccl.4.5-6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Eccl.4.5-6","value":"Eccl.4.5-6"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:5-6"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["Fools"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," do not even try to succeed and end up impoverished. Others frantically devote ",{"c":["two handfuls"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},"—all their time and effort—to ",{"c":["hard work"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," in pursuit of fleeting wealth that they will have no time to enjoy (cp. ",{"href":"?bref=Pr.23.4-5","r":"Pr.23.4-5","rawR":"Pr.23.4-5","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Pr.23.4-5","value":"Pr.23.4-5"}],"t":"ref","v":"Prov 23:4-5"},"). Wise people practice moderation and contentment (",{"c":["quietness"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Eccl.4.5-6","refs":[{"book":"ECC","endChapter":4,"endVerse":6,"startChapter":4,"startVerse":5}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Eccl.4.5-6","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"ECC","sourceChapterCoverage":[4],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"2ee0a249cf747196e7bd0688ab1d7f625656138ca3ef15a5b25d1e39e0021d61","sourceItemName":"Eccl.4.5-6","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":8010,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"ECC","canonicalOSIS":"Eccl","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Eccl"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Eccl.4.5-6","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Eccl.4.5-6","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Eccl.4.7-8","r":"Eccl.4.7-8","rawR":"Eccl.4.7-8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Eccl.4.7-8","value":"Eccl.4.7-8"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:7-8"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," This solitary man is a case study of the principle expressed in ",{"href":"?bref=Eccl.4.6","r":"Eccl.4.6","rawR":"Eccl.4.6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Eccl.4.6","value":"Eccl.4.6"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:6"},"."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Eccl.4.7-8","refs":[{"book":"ECC","endChapter":4,"endVerse":8,"startChapter":4,"startVerse":7}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Eccl.4.7-8","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"ECC","sourceChapterCoverage":[4],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"0e48500694c1ef499612461a0af79f1d3f7df62f4d4ee57ac151446134ea4739","sourceItemName":"Eccl.4.7-8","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":8011,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"ECC","canonicalOSIS":"Eccl","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Eccl"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Eccl.4.7-8","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Eccl.4.7-8","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Eccl.4.9-12","r":"Eccl.4.9-12","rawR":"Eccl.4.9-12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Eccl.4.9-12","value":"Eccl.4.9-12"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:9-12"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["Two people are better off than one:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," The solitary man (",{"href":"?bref=Eccl.4.8","r":"Eccl.4.8","rawR":"Eccl.4.8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Eccl.4.8","value":"Eccl.4.8"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:8"},") brings to mind the advantages of partnerships for business and for personal well-being."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Eccl.4.9-12","refs":[{"book":"ECC","endChapter":4,"endVerse":12,"startChapter":4,"startVerse":9}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Eccl.4.9-12","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"ECC","sourceChapterCoverage":[4],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"5867fbd8c65e7609e37a89b30ade82036333f2bf3486fb6180c95c868205434b","sourceItemName":"Eccl.4.9-12","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":8012,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"ECC","canonicalOSIS":"Eccl","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Eccl"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Eccl.4.9-12","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Eccl.4.9-12","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Eccl.4.13-14","r":"Eccl.4.13-14","rawR":"Eccl.4.13-14","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Eccl.4.13-14","value":"Eccl.4.13-14"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:13-14"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["It is better to be . . . wise:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," Wisdom is valuable for its practical benefits."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Eccl.4.13-14","refs":[{"book":"ECC","endChapter":4,"endVerse":14,"startChapter":4,"startVerse":13}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Eccl.4.13-14","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"ECC","sourceChapterCoverage":[4],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"67cebfaae4694651ceeac3bf437d76e47b36e918b8299c287dd7e73e97dd8452","sourceItemName":"Eccl.4.13-14","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":8013,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"ECC","canonicalOSIS":"Eccl","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Eccl"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Eccl.4.13-14","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Eccl.4.13-14","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Eccl.4.15-16","r":"Eccl.4.15-16","rawR":"Eccl.4.15-16","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Eccl.4.15-16","value":"Eccl.4.15-16"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:15-16"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," As with everything else, a ruler’s popularity is only temporary and fleeting, like ",{"c":["the wind"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},"."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Eccl.4.15-16","refs":[{"book":"ECC","endChapter":4,"endVerse":16,"startChapter":4,"startVerse":15}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Eccl.4.15-16","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"ECC","sourceChapterCoverage":[4],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"2d62b695b4e0e7f5159c545aa6bde147feb87547bae9a8aef3c201157e9bd923","sourceItemName":"Eccl.4.15-16","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":8014,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"ECC","canonicalOSIS":"Eccl","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Eccl"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Eccl.4.15-16","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Eccl.4.15-16","type":"StudyNote"}],"version":"v2-a101d843a2f0cd3fc0c6"}